{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML) is a disease in which the bone marrow makes too many white blood cells.","source_span":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia is a disease in which the bone marrow makes too many white blood cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"CML, also called chronic granulocytic leukemia, is a slowly progressing blood and bone marrow disease that usually occurs during or after middle age and rarely occurs in children.","source_span":"chronic myelogenous leukemia (also called CML or chronic granulocytic leukemia) is a slowly progressing blood and bone marrow disease that usually occurs during or after middle age, and rarely occurs in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"Signs and symptoms of CML include fever, night sweats, tiredness, weight loss, and pain or fullness below the ribs on the left side.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of chronic myelogenous leukemia include fever, night sweats, and tiredness."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"Most people with CML have a gene mutation called the Philadelphia chromosome.","source_span":"Most people with CML have a gene mutation (change) called the Philadelphia chromosome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"The Philadelphia chromosome causes the bone marrow to make an enzyme called tyrosine kinase, which causes too many stem cells to become white blood cells (granulocytes or blasts).","source_span":"It results in the bone marrow making an enzyme, called tyrosine kinase, that causes too many stem cells to become white blood cells (granulocytes or blasts)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"The Philadelphia chromosome is not passed from parent to child.","source_span":"The Philadelphia chromosome is not passed from parent to child."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for CML depend on the patient's age, the phase of CML, the amount of blasts in blood/bone marrow, the size of the spleen at diagnosis, and the patient's general health.","source_span":"The patients age. - The phase of CML. - The amount of blasts in the blood or bone marrow. - The size of the spleen at diagnosis. - The patients general health."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","statement":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia has 3 phases: chronic phase, accelerated phase, and blastic phase.","source_span":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia has 3 phases. - Chronic phase - Accelerated phase - Blastic phase"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What gene mutation do most people with chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML) have?","answer":"Most people with CML have a gene mutation called the Philadelphia chromosome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What enzyme does the Philadelphia chromosome cause the bone marrow to produce in CML, and what effect does it have?","answer":"It causes the bone marrow to produce an enzyme called tyrosine kinase, which causes too many stem cells to become white blood cells (granulocytes or blasts)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Is the Philadelphia chromosome in CML inherited from parent to child?","answer":"No, the Philadelphia chromosome is not passed from parent to child."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are the three phases of chronic myelogenous leukemia?","answer":"CML has three phases: the chronic phase, the accelerated phase, and the blastic phase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What symptoms might indicate chronic myelogenous leukemia?","answer":"Symptoms of CML include fever, night sweats, tiredness, weight loss for no known reason, and pain or fullness below the ribs on the left side."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What factors affect the prognosis and treatment options for a patient with CML?","answer":"Prognosis and treatment depend on the patient's age, the phase of CML, the amount of blasts in the blood or bone marrow, the size of the spleen at diagnosis, and the patient's general health."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_4","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"At what age does chronic myelogenous leukemia typically occur?","answer":"CML is a slowly progressing disease that usually occurs during or after middle age, and rarely occurs in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","statement":"Hairy cell leukemia is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes.","source_span":"Hairy cell leukemia is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many lymphocytes (a type of white blood cell)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","statement":"Hairy cell leukemia is named for how the leukemia cells look \"hairy\" when viewed under a microscope.","source_span":"The disease is called hairy cell leukemia because the leukemia cells look \"hairy\" when viewed under a microscope."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","statement":"Hairy cell leukemia is a rare type of leukemia that gets worse slowly or does not get worse at all.","source_span":"This rare type of leukemia gets worse slowly or does not get worse at all."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","statement":"The cause of hairy cell leukemia is unknown, and it occurs more often in older men.","source_span":"The cause of hairy cell leukemia is unknown. It occurs more often in older men."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","statement":"Signs and symptoms of hairy cell leukemia include infections, tiredness, and pain below the ribs, as well as weakness, fever, easy bruising or bleeding, shortness of breath, weight loss, and painless lumps.","source_span":"Signs and symptoms of hairy cell leukemia include infections, tiredness, and pain below the ribs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","statement":"A BRAF gene mutation is often found in patients with hairy cell leukemia.","source_span":"A BRAF gene mutation is often found in patients with hairy cell leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","statement":"Some leukemia cells in hairy cell leukemia may collect in the spleen and cause it to swell.","source_span":"Some of the leukemia cells may collect in the spleen and cause it to swell."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Why is hairy cell leukemia called \"hairy\" cell leukemia?","answer":"It is named because the leukemia cells look \"hairy\" when viewed under a microscope."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Who is at higher risk of developing hairy cell leukemia, and is the cause known?","answer":"The cause of hairy cell leukemia is unknown, but it occurs more often in older men."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are common signs and symptoms of hairy cell leukemia?","answer":"Symptoms include infections, tiredness, pain below the ribs, weakness, fever, easy bruising or bleeding, shortness of breath, weight loss, and painless lumps in the neck, underarm, stomach, or groin."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Does hairy cell leukemia always cause noticeable symptoms?","answer":"No, hairy cell leukemia can sometimes present with symptoms like infections, tiredness, and pain below the ribs, but it isn't stated to always be symptomatic in every patient — symptoms listed are what to check with a doctor about."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which gene mutation is commonly found in patients with hairy cell leukemia?","answer":"A BRAF gene mutation is often found in patients with hairy cell leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_5","fact_refs":[0,2],"question":"What is hairy cell leukemia and how quickly does it progress?","answer":"It is a rare cancer of the blood and bone marrow in which too many lymphocytes are made; it gets worse slowly or does not get worse at all."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"Childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many immature lymphocytes.","source_span":"Childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many immature lymphocytes (a type of white blood cell)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"ALL is the most common type of cancer in children.","source_span":"ALL is the most common type of cancer in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"Childhood ALL usually gets worse quickly if it is not treated.","source_span":"This type of cancer usually gets worse quickly if it is not treated."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"Past treatment for cancer and certain genetic conditions affect the risk of having childhood ALL.","source_span":"Past treatment for cancer and certain genetic conditions affect the risk of having childhood ALL."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"Signs of childhood ALL include fever, easy bruising or bleeding, petechiae, bone or joint pain, painless lumps, pain below the ribs, weakness, and loss of appetite.","source_span":"Signs of childhood ALL include fever and bruising."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"In Philadelphia chromosome positive ALL, part of one chromosome switches places with part of another chromosome.","source_span":"in Philadelphia chromosome positive ALL, part of one chromosome switches places with part of another chromosome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"A lumbar puncture, also called an LP or spinal tap, collects a sample of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) to check for signs that leukemia cells have spread to the brain and spinal cord.","source_span":"This procedure is also called an LP or spinal tap."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","statement":"Intrathecal chemotherapy is given after the CSF sample is removed to treat any leukemia cells that may have spread to the brain and spinal cord.","source_span":"Intrathecal chemotherapy is given after the sample of fluid is removed to treat any leukemia cells that may have spread to the brain and spinal cord."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is the most common type of cancer in children?","answer":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is the most common type of cancer in children."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What factors affect the risk of a child developing acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","answer":"Past treatment for cancer and certain genetic conditions affect the risk of having childhood ALL."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is another name for the lumbar puncture procedure used in diagnosing childhood ALL, and what does it check for?","answer":"It is also called an LP or spinal tap; it collects cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) to check whether leukemia cells have spread to the brain and spinal cord."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What treatment is given after a CSF sample is removed during a lumbar puncture in childhood ALL, and why?","answer":"Intrathecal chemotherapy is given after the fluid sample is removed, to treat any leukemia cells that may have spread to the brain and spinal cord."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What happens to the chromosomes in Philadelphia chromosome positive childhood ALL?","answer":"Part of one chromosome switches places with part of another chromosome."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_6","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are common signs of childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","answer":"Signs include fever, easy bruising or bleeding, petechiae, bone or joint pain, painless lumps in the neck/underarm/stomach/groin, pain below the ribs, weakness, and loss of appetite."}
